  and authorizes Google to do so but the  idea is that it breathes new commercial  life into these out of print books many  of which can only be found on the  shelves of some major research libraries  here in the United States further the  settlement allows for an authorization  based scheme through the class action  mechanism the rights holders are  authorizing google to display books to  index books to make internal research  uses of books and essentially to scan  books and that this scheme of  authorization through the class action  mechanism is consistent with the  copyright owners view and it is to be  sure very difficult in any other context  other than a class action to get rights  from this large body of rights holders  particularly of out of print books  further the settlement provides new  revenue models or methods for making  these books available essentially as I  say injecting commercial life into the  books and the  the through there are three business  models the first is that consumers can  purchase access to a book online from  any computer connected to the Internet  in the United States second that there  are institutional subscriptions in which  institutions such as this university can  buy access to the entire corpus of books  and make those books available to  students faculty researchers and others  there's an advertising based model and  then there are other display uses that  Google can make such as preview use that  is to say that anyone can see probably  up to twenty percent of a book for free  based on their search terms and then  there are further business models that  the settlement contemplates but are not  yet approved at this time such as PDF  download and print on demand and so  forth all of these revenue models  contemplate a 6337 split sixty-three  percent to the rights holder and  thirty-seven percent to google that was  considered and is considered to be a  very favorable revenue split from the  content owners standpoint the sett